+2. **The provisional bound applies from the wait's start.** At every moment of a wait the deadline is the fixed bound once it is set, and the provisional bound (`(SLOT_BUILDER_CAP + SLOT_GRACE)` scaled, from the wait's start) until then. A wait in which no live record is ever read ends at the provisional bound with `bridge-run: slot not free - held by unknown`, then `bridge-run: exit 123` last. The fixing rule of round 1's Requirement 5 is unchanged (the first live record whose end can be computed fixes the bound once: its end, never earlier than the wait's start plus the waiter's own kind's default, never later than the provisional bound; never recomputed). Test, by launch: a helper that takes the lock and never publishes a record; a waiter with `BRIDGE_RUN_SLOT_SCALE` small enough that the provisional bound is a few seconds exits 123 at about that bound (within the lock's one-second polling), its only waiting lines being exactly `bridge-run: waiting for the slot held by unknown`, its last two lines `bridge-run: slot not free - held by unknown` and `bridge-run: exit 123`, its command never started (a marker file it would write is absent).
+3. **The wait's clock is monotonic.** The wait's start, its deadline and its reporting interval run on `time.monotonic()`, so a wall clock set back or forward does not stretch or cut a wait. The record's times (`start`, its `<end>`) are wall-clock UTC and are compared with the wall clock once, when the bound is fixed. `waited` stays the whole seconds of the wait. Test: in-process or by launch, the test you choose that fails when the deadline is taken from `time.time()` (for example with `time.time` patched to jump during a wait in-process); name it in the mutation table.
+4. **Liveness on Windows needs no child process.** On Windows a record's process is alive only when `OpenProcess` on its pid succeeds and the handle says it has not exited (`GetExitCodeProcess` returns `STILL_ACTIVE`, or `WaitForSingleObject(h, 0)` returns `WAIT_TIMEOUT`), and its creation time from the same or another handle equals the record's within the existing tolerance. No `tasklist` and no other child process is started by the slot's code, on any path; every handle is closed on every path. On POSIX, `os.kill(pid, 0)`'s `OverflowError` and `ValueError` are caught and read as not alive (besides the pid range of Requirement 5). Test, in-process: a record naming a live helper's pid and its true creation time reads live; the same record with the creation time off by one second reads as no record (this is round 1's surviving "liveness ignores the creation time" mutant); the same record after the helper is killed and waited for reads as no record; and the launcher's source holds no `tasklist` (an assertion on the program's text).
+5. **The record is validated before any use.** A record reads as no record, without an exception escaping, unless all of these hold: it is a JSON object; `kind` is a `str` and is among `KINDS`; `run_id` is a `str` of the launcher's run-id form with ASCII digits only (`[0-9]` or `re.ASCII`), a `-N` suffix allowed; `start` is a `str` of the plain `slot taken` form `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ` with ASCII digits only and **no** `-N` suffix, parsed whole by `datetime.strptime` (never by slicing or `split`), a real date not before 2000-01-01 and not later than now plus a small clock skew (a named constant of at most 300 s; state the value in the report); `pid` is an exact `int` (`type(x) is int`, so not `bool`) with 1 <= pid <= 4,294,967,295 on Windows and 1 <= pid <= 4,194,304 elsewhere; `limit` is an exact `int`, not `bool`, with 0 < limit <= 604,800 (one week, a named constant; not `SLOT_BUILDER_CAP`, because a holder whose limit the owner raised with `Ceiling above twice:` publishes a limit above 14,400 and must still be named in the waiting line; the wait's bound is capped by the provisional bound whatever the record's limit); `created` is an `int` or a `float`, not `bool`, and finite; no string field holds a control character; and the process is alive with that creation time (Requirement 4). Every computation from the record (the end time, `fromtimestamp`, the bound) is guarded so that `OverflowError`, `OSError` and `ValueError` give no record or `unknown`, never a traceback. Test: Requirement 17(a).
+6. **A lock error other than contention is not a held slot.** On Windows only the contention errnos (`EACCES`, `EDEADLOCK`) read as "held"; on POSIX only `BlockingIOError`. Any other error while opening the state folder or the lock file, or while locking, ends the run before its command starts, with exit 125 and the existing line `bridge-run: refused - ~/.cursor-bridge/heavy.lock (<class>)` (the error's class name, never the absolute path). Test: by launch, with `heavy.lock` made a folder in the temporary home (the open fails): exit 125, a line matching that form exactly, no absolute path of the temporary home in stderr, the command never started (marker absent), no traceback; and in-process, a lock attempt on a descriptor that is not open (it fails with `EBADF`) is not reported as "held".
+7. **The slot is released by the launcher's exit.** On the normal path and on the 124 path the launcher never unlocks or closes the lock explicitly; the operating system releases it when the launcher process ends, after its last line, its log entry and (on Windows) with the Job. The 125 path after a failed `Popen` may unlock. Test: the diff shows no unlock or close of the lock outside the failed-`Popen` paths (say so in the report); Requirement 17(e)'s killed-holder test shows the release on process end.
+8. **The waiting line names the most recent live record read.** Each waiting line names the last live record the waiter has read (`last_live`), not only a record read on that poll: once a live record has been read, a later poll that reads no live record (the file removed, replaced by garbage, or naming a dead process) still prints the last live record's kind, run id, `since` and `ends by`. Before any live record has been read the line is exactly `bridge-run: waiting for the slot held by unknown`. Test: Requirement 17(d) (after the replacement record is read, a further replacement by text that does not parse leaves the waiting lines naming the last live record).
+9. **The docstring's order of lines is true.** The module docstring states where the slot's lines come: after `bridge-run: started <run id>`, the launch's own notes, the limit line and the other launch lines, and before the command starts: the waiting lines, then `bridge-run: slot taken <UTC time>Z`, or `bridge-run: slot not free - ...` and `bridge-run: exit 123`. It names `waited`, the `"slot": "not free"` log field, and `BRIDGE_RUN_SLOT_SCALE` as test-only and honoured only beside `BRIDGE_RUN_ALLOW_ANY=1`. Test: `python tools/dump-cli.py --check` after regeneration; the reviewer reads the sentences.
